// Hypothesis:
//   Overpass-the-hash (a.k.a. pass-the-key) turns a stolen NTLM hash into a
//   Kerberos TGT, letting an attacker "upgrade" to Kerberos and move laterally
//   while blending into normal domain traffic. Tools that do this (e.g. Mimikatz
//   sekurlsa::pth) request the TGT with RC4-HMAC encryption (0x17). In a modern
//   AES-only domain, a user-account TGT issued with RC4 is anomalous and a
//   high-signal indicator of overpass-the-hash.
//
// Data source:
//   - Windows Security Event Log 4768 (Kerberos TGT / AS-REQ issued)
//
// Field notes:
//   - TicketEncryptionType: 0x17 = RC4-HMAC (legacy), 0x12 = AES256, 0x11 = AES128.
//   - Machine accounts (name ends with "$") are excluded: they legitimately fall
//     back to RC4 in some configurations and would generate noise.

authentication where
  event.code == "4768" and
  winlog.event_data.TicketEncryptionType == "0x17" and
  not endsWith(winlog.event_data.TargetUserName, "$") and
  not winlog.event_data.TargetUserName in ("krbtgt", "-")
